\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.65\textwidth]{figures/cka_analysis.pdf}
\caption{CKA similarity between fine-tuned and pretrained encoder features. All conditions except Detach OCC preserve pretrained structure (CKA $\geq$0.95), including Full OCC+LN, which achieves the lowest CAR despite high CKA (a representation-behavior dissociation). Detach OCC drifts catastrophically, especially in SigLIP middle layers, which confirms BatchNorm as the sole cause of representational drift.}
\label{fig:cka_analysis}
\end{figure}

\textbf{CKA confirms BN as the sole cause of representational drift; however, representations do not predict behavior.} CKA between fine-tuned and pretrained features (Figure~\ref{fig:cka_analysis}) shows that all non-Detach-BN conditions preserve pretrained structure (CKA $\geq$ 0.95): Vanilla, Full OCC, Random OCC, Detach+LN, and Full OCC+LN. Detach OCC alone drifts catastrophically (DINOv2: 0.539; SigLIP: 0.312). CKA analysis indicates that LN generally does not cause representational drift (s1, s2 CKA${\approx}$0.97), suggesting that the CAR degradation under Full OCC+LN operates through gradient dynamics (specifically the cross-backbone gradient redistribution described above) rather than representation corruption. Yet Full OCC+LN achieves the \emph{lowest} CAR (0.743) while Full OCC with BN achieves the highest (0.797), both at high CKA. This \emph{representation-behavior dissociation} demonstrates that preserving pretrained features is neither sufficient nor predictive of collision avoidance.

